<td id="S3.T1.2.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1,142</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Total utterances for each dialogue in our dataset created from the CReST corpus. This includes all of the dialogues from the corpus which have a corresponding video, which is necessary in order to establish ground truth annotations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We generated our dataset (Step 1 in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Annotation Procedure ‣ 3 Dataset: The CReST Corpus ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) by
having three groups of annotators label the same six dialogues for the
searcher’s and director’s beliefs, goals, and commitments. The first
set of annotators was a series of naive LLMs which were naive as they
were only given limited information about the environment: prompts
detailed task overview (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Full Prompts ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for prompts) but there was
no plausible way for the LLM to establish a ground truth understanding
of the environment operating on natural language interaction
alone. The second set of annotators were two naive humans who were
given the same information as the LLMs. Human annotators each
independently annotated the dialogues, then, to abjudicate, compared
annotations and agreed on their correct interpretation of the
dialogue. In this way, we are able to compare the LLM and human
annotations with the ground truth later on to identify the differences
between naive LLM and human annotations. Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Annotation Procedure ‣ 3 Dataset: The CReST Corpus ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
shows the annotation structure for each utterance, which was employed
by both human and LLM annotators.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02292v1/ann_structure.jpeg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/531;" width="476" height="531" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Using an example from Dialogue 1, we show the annotation structure used by LLMs to identify shared mental models and the common beliefs held by agents. Our dataset of annotations includes an annotation of this structure for each utterance.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the third set of annotations, we employed two humans to annotate
in the same way, but this time with access to a video which provides
ground truth knowledge. Each dialogue corresponded to a headcam video
from the searcher’s point-of-view, the director’s screen with the map,
and audio between the two. This set of annotations, corroborated by
three humans, serves as our ground truth as we analyze the role of an
LLM as an annotator later on.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Following the creation of our dataset, we were able to move on to Step
2 of Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Annotation Procedure ‣ 3 Dataset: The CReST Corpus ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the annotation discrepancy analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02292v1/OverviewFigure.jpeg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/234;" width="476" height="234" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>An overview of our two-step process used to annotate and find discrepancies between SMMs using LLMs. We are able to create two outputs: (1) a dataset of ground truth, human, and LLM annotations and (2) a dataset of discrepancies between ground truth annotations and human/LLM annotations. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discrepancy Analysis Framework</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are two separate applications of LLMs in this work: one where an
LLM annotates, and the second where an LLM finds discrepancies between
sets of annotations. For the latter, the question arises how to detect
and measure potential discrepancies among complex mental models
identified in simple natural dialogue. We, therefore, first introduce
our discrepancy framework and metric, then apply it to our dataset.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          We will make the entire dataset available on Github for external use when the paper is published.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Discrepancy Framework</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have developed an initial comparison framework for SMM annotations
that can be used to determine the extent to which two annotations
agree and are coherent. This framework and metric can then be used
for comparing any two mental annotations, whether they be obtained
from humans or artificial agents. The aim of the framework was to
identify not only how many discrepancies are present between two
mental annotations, but also to define the “severity” of these
discrepancies, from mere omissions of facts in one, to
incompatibilities of beliefs, to false beliefs compared to ground
truth, second order false beliefs, and so forth. All of these are
different forms of discrepancies that can occur in any combination
between two mental annotations with different implications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework identifies four distinct types of discrepancies, each
characterized by specific definitions, severities, and use
cases: <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">belief contradictions, omissions, unsupported beliefs,
and false beliefs</span>. The following section presents each in turn,
following this order. To do so, we use an example where new elements are introduced to
showcase our different discrepancies simultaneously coexisting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Belief Contradictions</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first type of discrepancy is a <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">belief contradiction</span>, where one
annotator believes something and another annotator believes its negation.
If, for example, the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ground truth</span> states that there exists a
pink box in the room and the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">annotator</span>
specifically believes that the pink box is <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span>
there, we have identified a belief
contradiction. This ground-level disagreement specifically must
maintain internal consistency, in this case with the searcher and
director. Their contradiction comes from opposing beliefs about the
state of the world. From an analysis perspective, these discrepancies
are quite severe and can commonly arise, yet are distinct from the
other following categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Omissions</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second case of the levels of discrepancies is omissions. While
more self-explanatory, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">omissions</span> are defined as a clear
discrepancy where one annotator failed to identify a belief which was
identified in the ground truth annotation. Discrepancies may be an
omission in one annotation while a belief contradiction in another,
given context. For example, if the annotator
in our example says nothing
about the pink box, then it would qualify as an omission rather than a
belief contradiction. Our annotations have many examples of omissions,
as the LLM often identified true beliefs that were noncomprehensive.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3 </span>Unsupported Beliefs</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unsupported beliefs</span> are defined as beliefs that an annotator identifies but are not contradicted nor supported
in the ground truth or by another annotator i.e. they may not be validated nor negated. They are characterized by their seemingly sporadic appearances and often function as hallucinations by an LLM annotator. If an LLM added an annotation that
the searcher’s goal is to follow the director’s directions, it isn’t
necessarily incorrect but is not grounded in any dialogue, nor is it relevant. This kind
of inference may be harmless, but could be applied to much more severe
situations. For example, if an LLM annotator hallucination results in an unsupported belief
and given agency in the task, it would greatly impact team performance
and safety.
Hence, this unsupported belief requires further investigation and must
be noted as well, though it is qualitatively the inverse of a simple
omission because it cannot be validated using the ground
truth. Unsupported beliefs may be false or a belief contradiction, as
well as combinations thereof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4 </span>False Beliefs</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False beliefs</span> are defined as simply
incorrect annotations according to an established ground truth. This would occur if the human annotator writes that the searcher
believes that there is a green box in the room, while the ground truth
identifies a pink box in the room. These are similar to belief contradictions in that
they have the same result, a direct contradiction. However, belief contradictions more specifically
identify situations where the positive and negated beliefs are both present, whereas false beliefs
are incoherent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As we see examples of all of the above discrepancies between humans
and LLMs and the ground truth, these discrepancies should be part of
any metric measuring the divergence of two mental annotations. In
addition, we must also identify the severity of these discrepancies as
they pertain to annotation coherence as well as team coherence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Severity of Discrepancies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we introduce a general ranking of the discrepancies identified
in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Discrepancy Framework ‣ 4 Discrepancy Analysis Framework ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> that can be adapted for task specifics.
For example, omissions and extraneous facts might not matter for some
tasks while for others those might lead to performance degradations
and thus will be critical for team success. In some tasks, second
order false beliefs of a team member might not matter (e.g., if the
person is simply performing task that does not depend on what others
are doing), while in other cases it will be essential (e.g., in triage
settings when two team members both wrongly believe that the other
person would tend to the wounded victim).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reflect these contextual differences, we rank discrepancies in
descending order of severity as follows: Belief Contradictions, False
Beliefs, Unsupported Beliefs, Omissions. In practice, False Beliefs
are as critical as Belief Contradictions when a ground truth is
available, but otherwise become unmeasurable. Unsupported Beliefs may
reflect model hallucination but are not necessarily harmful unless
they mislead downstream decisions. Omissions are the least severe,
though omitting key state updates can still degrade task performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To operationalize this severity ranking, we define a weighted discrepancy score per model–dialogue pair <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,d)</annotation></semantics></math>. Discrepancies may be weighed different amounts at the discretion of the analyzer depending on the corpus and task used. For alternative applications of this metric, one may place more weight in a specific discrepancy type due to its application to a scenario, and therefore choose weights accordingly. We define this weighted metric as follows.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> = number of Belief Contradictions</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> = number of False Beliefs</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> = number of Unsupported Beliefs</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> = number of Omissions</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> = weight for respective discrepancy <math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the weighted raw discrepancy score is</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{m,d}=w_{b}B_{m,d}+w_{f}F_{m,d}+w_{u}U_{m,d}+w_{o}O_{m,d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{m,d}=w_{b}B_{m,d}+w_{f}F_{m,d}+w_{u}U_{m,d}+w_{o}O_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To account for dialogue length, we divide by the number of utterances <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in dialogue <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in a per-utterance discrepancy score:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m,d}=\frac{r_{m,d}}{N_{d}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m,d}=\frac{r_{m,d}}{N_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to enable fair comparisons across models and dialogues, we normalize the scores to the range <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{m,d}=1-\frac{s_{m,d}-s_{\min}}{s_{\max}-s_{\min}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{m,d}=1-\frac{s_{m,d}-s_{\min}}{s_{\max}-s_{\min}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the minimum and maximum <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> values across all <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,d)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs. A higher <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates better alignment with ground truth, i.e., fewer and/or less severe discrepancies per utterance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">This severity-informed metric provides a principled way to compare annotation quality and model reasoning performance across dialogue complexities.</p>
</div>
