<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>LLM Discrepancy analysis results across 6 dialogues for each of 4 discrepancy types. Each row indicates the number of a discrepancy type identified for a given dialogue. The same LLM model was run as a discrepancy identifier as its annotator and human naive annotations were run using Claude Sonnet 4. <span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Blue</span> numbers highlight the lowest total number of discrepancies in a dialogue across all annotators and <span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">purple</span> numbers highlight the highest total discrepancies in a certain dialogue across all annotators.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To experiment with this new form of annotation and ToM reasoning tasks using LLMs, we employed a secondary LLM to analyze annotations 1:1 for each utterance of our annotations. This section corresponds to Step 2 in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Annotation Procedure ‣ 3 Dataset: The CReST Corpus ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and results in our second output of a dataset of specifically identified discrepancies, which we label and total in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Severity of Discrepancies ‣ 4 Discrepancy Analysis Framework ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we used our dataset of six annotated CReST dialogues, each containing naive human, naive LLM, and ground truth annotations. To assess reproducibility, we tested multiple LLMs using the prompt shown in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Full Prompts ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Given the size of our dataset, we constrained model selection to those with established popularity and prior evidence of strong performance in order to minimize environmental impact (see <a href="#S8" title="In LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">8 </span>Ethical Considerations</span></a>). Each model’s annotations were evaluated by a separate LLM evaluator against the same ground truth annotation, and also compared naive human annotations to the ground truth. This setup allowed us to assess both the annotation capabilities and discrepancy detection abilities of each model, as all evaluators performed the same secondary comparison. To investigate the effectiveness of the LLMs as discrepancy identifiers, we conducted human validation of D1, shown in the Appendix’s <a href="#A3" title="In LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">Appendix C </span>Validation of Discrepancy Identification</span></a>. Our objective was to determine which models most effectively produced SMM-aligned annotations, demonstrated understanding of our discrepancy framework, and adhered to its guidelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We report discrepancy counts across four categories: <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">belief contradictions</span>, <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omissions</span>, <span id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupported beliefs</span>, and <span id="S5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false beliefs</span> for three LLMs and a naive human baseline, each evaluated against gold-standard annotations across six situated CReST dialogues.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02292v1/total_disc.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Visualization of the total discrepancies identified by each LLM. It is clear that Sonnet 4 identifies a large number more than other models, which as we discuss in Results, could be a positive or negative depending on how it is used. Visualizations of specific discrepancy types may be found in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Discrepancy Visualizations ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Discrepancy Trends</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Severity of Discrepancies ‣ 4 Discrepancy Analysis Framework ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Discrepancy Trends ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarize total discrepancies per annotator across all six dialogues. Across both a basic counting metric and normalized weighted score, we see the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:10pt;">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4</span> exhibits the highest overall discrepancy counts, surpassing 700 discrepancies in Dialogue 3 and having consistently the lowest discrepancy score across all dialogues as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Discrepancy Trends ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The model frequently introduces <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupported beliefs</span> and <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">belief contradictions</span>, suggesting a tendency toward overgeneralized or speculative reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-mini</span> and <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma 8.5B</span> produce markedly fewer discrepancies, but with distinct profiles. o3-mini displays high <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omission</span> rates and numerous <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupported beliefs</span>, while Gemma, though conservative on omissions, identifies substantial <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">belief contradictions</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Naive human annotators</span> demonstrate a discrepancy distribution that overlaps considerably with LLM profiles, particularly in <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">belief contradictions</span> and <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omissions</span>, but outperform LLMs in limiting unsupported beliefs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across all annotators, certain dialogues (D3 and D5) consistently elicit higher discrepancy counts, indicating increased annotation difficulty, likely due to their longer lengths and therefore increased spatial ambiguity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:98.3pt;vertical-align:-46.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(23.0pt,-6.6pt) scale(1.15400492676611,1.15400492676611) ;">
