<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Summary of normalized discrepancy scores <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{m,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{m,d}</annotation></semantics></math> for each model across six dialogues with <math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> set to 1 for all weights. Bolded values represent the highest for each dialogue. Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Discrepancy Details ‣ LLMs and their Limited Theory of Mind: Evaluating Mental State Annotations in Situated Dialogue" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> shows more discrepancy specifics.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Comparison to Human Annotations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Naive human annotations contain fewer unsupported beliefs than any LLM, but comparable or greater numbers of belief contradictions and omissions. This suggests that, while humans are better at avoiding hallucinated or unjustified inferences, they are also vulnerable to erroneous inferences about task state or agent perspective when lacking access to environmental context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of discrepancy counts, the model whose profile most closely matches human annotators is <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma 8.5B</span>. However, this similarity has a trade off: while Gemma avoids omissions, it more frequently makes incorrect inferences about ground truth belief states.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Implications for Situated Theory of Mind</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These findings highlight the nuanced but limited capacity of current LLMs to simulate ToM-like inference in grounded, task-based dialogue. Specifically:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:10pt;">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs may be capable of continuously representing internally consistent belief structures via dialogue but often fail to correctly ground those beliefs in environmental constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The prevalence of <span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupported beliefs</span> and <span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contradictions</span> in models like Claude points to limitations in evidence prioritization and contextual anchoring.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dominance of <span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omissions</span> in o3-mini and the absence thereof in Gemma suggest divergent strategies in annotation scope, under-specification versus overcommitment, across models.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While LLMs demonstrate surface-level ToM capabilities in terms of belief attribution and second-order mental state modeling, their performance remains qualitatively distinct from that of humans. They are designed to be driven more by linguistic regularity than grounded situational reasoning. These discrepancies underscore the importance of hybrid pipelines (e.g., LLM+human validation) and the need for more cognitively grounded architectures for ToM-sensitive tasks. Still, they provide a useful framework by which annotation may be sped up and validated, as they are able to utilize their natural language skills to analyze annotations and SMMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion &amp; Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study situates LLMs at a critical juncture: capable of simulating mental state attribution, yet ultimately constrained by a lack of embodied reasoning and grounded situational awareness. Our results suggest that while LLMs can emulate surface-level features of ToM, their representations remain separate from the kinds of inferential integration including spatial, temporal, and epistemic reasoning, that underlie human teamwork.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This limitation is not merely a technical deficiency but reflects a broader epistemological gap: our results suggest that current LLMs approximate understanding through patterns in language rather than through participation in the physical and social worlds those patterns describe. As such, they risk appearing competent in annotation while failing in intervention, which is precisely the point at which team coherence depends most on shared mental models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The implications of this work are twofold. First, from a cognitive modeling perspective, situated dialogue offers a richer and more ecologically valid benchmark for ToM than abstract question-answering tasks. It foregrounds the dynamic, perspectival reasoning essential for coordination. Second, from an engineering standpoint, our framework identifies where and how LLMs break down in multi-agent alignment, which is information that’s critical for designing collaborative AI systems. Ultimately, enabling LLMs to function as coherent teammates will require not just more data, but cognitively grounded models capable of representing belief, perspective, and intention in context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are a few limitations of our experiments, which largely lie in the reliance on the nature of LLMs. As it is now well known, LLMs introduce variability due to model updates, non-deterministic outputs, and restricted control over underlying architectures. While we evaluated multiple models from different companies, prompt sensitivity may have influenced results slightly, though we do account for this by testing multiple dialogues with similar results and one locally run model. We also observe divergence across models even with three models, showing the point that our models are largely representative of the most widely used LLMs. Additionally, our experimental setup was constrained by the scale of the CReST corpus subset (six dialogues) and the cost of running multiple passes for annotation and discrepancy detection. Future experiments may include more comparisons across more diverse dialogue contexts and larger model ensembles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In selecting models for this study, we prioritized a balance between experimental rigor and environmental responsibility. Large-scale evaluation across dozens of LLMs would have significantly increased compute time and energy consumption, contributing to the growing carbon footprint associated with LLM research. Instead, we chose a representative sample of three high-performing models from major LLM paradigms: OpenAI’s o3-mini (proprietary autoregressive transformer), Anthropic’s Claude (Constitutional AI and safety-aligned), and Gemma (locally run and smaller at 8.5B parameters). These selections reflect a diverse cross-section of architectures, alignment strategies, and interface styles, providing meaningful comparative insights without unnecessary ecological impact. It is the belief of the authors that there is not a need to exhaust environmental resources when in fact our results show similar trends across even these three vastly different models. We encourage future work to adopt similar principled selection criteria to mitigate environmental harm while still advancing scientific understanding.</p>
</div>
</section>
